-- title:
--   $\mathcal T$ is generated by nearest-neighbour $XX$ and $ZZ$
-- statement:
--   Let $n\ge1$ and let $\mathcal T$ be the group of Pauli operators on the $n\times n$ lattice with an even number of $X$'s in every column and an even number of $Z$'s in every row. Then $\mathcal T$ is generated by the nearest-neighbour operators
--
--   $$
--   \mathcal T = \big\langle X_{i,j}X_{i+1,j},\ Z_{j,i}Z_{j,i+1}\ :\ i\in\mathbb Z_{n-1},\ j\in\mathbb Z_n\big\rangle .
--   $$
--
--   Modulo phase: a pair $(a,b)$ satisfies all column parities $e_j(a)=0$ and row parities $f_i(b)=0$ if and only if it lies in the $\mathbb Z_2$-span of the vertical pairs $X_{i,j}X_{i+1,j}$ and the horizontal pairs $Z_{j,i}Z_{j,i+1}$. In particular these operators form a group.
--
--   These two-qubit generators are the gauge operators that the self-correcting Hamiltonian of the paper is built from.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: lattice size n, rows and columns indexed 1..n, generators indexed by Z_{n−1} → n = m + 1 with m : ℕ; rows and columns Fin (m + 1), 0-based; generators indexed by Fin m acting on i.castSucc, i.succ; reason: Lean convention; n = 0 has no qubits, so every lattice of the paper is an instance
--   - encoding: Pauli operators with phases ±1, ±i (the (−1)^φ of (8), (13)) → pairs (a, b) of bit matrices modulo phase; multiplication is addition, commutation is the symplectic bit symp; reason: membership in T, S, C(S), commutation and weight do not depend on the phase
--   - reading: "nearest neighbor operators on our cubic lattice" → the n × n square lattice of §IV; reason: §IV is two-dimensional; "cubic" is a slip with no content
-- source:
--   D. Bacon, Operator Quantum Error Correcting Subsystems for Self-Correcting Quantum Memories, arXiv:quant-ph/0506023v4, §IV.A, eqs. (8)–(9), p.4

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_InT

namespace BaconOQEC

/-- Bacon, *Operator Quantum Error Correcting Subsystems for Self-Correcting Quantum Memories*,
arXiv:quant-ph/0506023v4, §IV.A, eqs. (8)–(9), p.4: "Note that these operators form a group
under multiplication. This group can be generated by nearest neighbor operators on our cubic
lattice T = ⟨X_{i,j}X_{i+1,j}, Z_{j,i}Z_{j,i+1}, ∀i ∈ Z_{n−1}, j ∈ Z_n⟩." The Pauli operators of
`T` (modulo phase) are exactly the `Z₂`-span of the vertical nearest-neighbour pairs
`X_{i,j}X_{i+1,j}` and the horizontal nearest-neighbour pairs `Z_{j,i}Z_{j,i+1}`.

**Formalization Note.** Indices are 0-based (`Fin (m + 1)`, lattice size `n = m + 1`, so `n ≥ 1`); Pauli operators
modulo phase are pairs `(a, b)` of bit matrices, and multiplication modulo phase is addition of pairs, so the
generated group is the `ZMod 2`-span; being a span, `T` is closed under multiplication ("form a
group"). Generator indices `i : Fin m` (pairs `i.castSucc`, `i.succ`) and `j : Fin (m + 1)`. The
signs `(−1)^φ` of (8) are dropped. ("cubic lattice" is a slip for the square lattice.) -/
theorem T_eq_span (m : ℕ) (a b : Bits m) :
    InT a b ↔ (a, b) ∈ Submodule.span (ZMod 2)
      ({v : Bits m × Bits m | ∃ (i : Fin m) (j : Fin (m + 1)),
          v = (fun r c => if (r = i.castSucc ∨ r = i.succ) ∧ c = j then 1 else 0, 0)} ∪
        {v : Bits m × Bits m | ∃ (i : Fin m) (j : Fin (m + 1)),
          v = (0, fun r c => if r = j ∧ (c = i.castSucc ∨ c = i.succ) then 1 else 0)}) := by sorry

end BaconOQEC
